  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "The observed effect might be confounded by strategic choices, team offensive schemes, or specific player combinations. Teams that frequently use drives to open up other actions might naturally have higher PNR PPP for other reasons (e.g., better offensive talent, coaching strategy). Without controlling for these factors, any observed correlation may not be causal."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "Depending on the precise definition of 'preceding' and the frequency of such sequences, the sample size for 'PNR plays preceded by a teammate DRIVE' might be limited, especially when breaking down by specific players, teams, or game situations, potentially leading to insufficient statistical power."
  },
  {
   "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
   "text": "The reliability of the 'preceding' relationship depends on the precision of the play-type classifier and its ability to correctly identify sequences of actions or possessions. Any misclassification or ambiguity in play-type assignment could introduce noise."
  },
  {
   "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
   "text": "If 'preceding' implies a very short time window or within-possession actions, ensuring that the 'DRIVE' is truly antecedent and not part of the PNR itself (or an alternative action that makes the PNR less effective) requires careful temporal definition to avoid leakage."
  }
 ],
